## Topics Covered

### 1.4 Cryptographic Solutions

**Symmetric vs Asymmetric Encryption**
- Symmetric — same key encrypts and decrypts, fast, key distribution problem at scale
- Asymmetric — public key encrypts, private key decrypts, solves distribution problem
- In practice both are used together — asymmetric to exchange a key, symmetric for data

**Session Keys**
- Temporary symmetric key generated for one session then discarded
- Perfect Forward Secrecy — past sessions protected even if key is later compromised

**Key Exchange**
- Out-of-band — physically transfer key, not practical for real-time internet
- In-band — use asymmetric encryption to transfer a symmetric key securely
- Key exchange algorithms (e.g. Diffie-Hellman) — derive same symmetric key on both
  sides without ever sending it across the network

**Hashing**
- One-way fingerprint of data, cannot be reversed
- SHA-256 — current standard, 256-bit output, 64 hex characters
- MD5 — collision vulnerability found 1996, deprecated
- SHA-1 — also deprecated, collision issues
- Salt — random data added to password before hashing, defeats rainbow tables
- Key stretching — hash the hash multiple times to slow brute force

**Digital Signatures**
- Hash encrypted with sender's private key
- Proves integrity (data unchanged) and origin (came from sender)
- Provides nonrepudiation — sender cannot deny sending it
- Verify with sender's public key

**PKI and Certificates**
- X.509 format — standard digital certificate format
- Certificate Authority (CA) — signs certificates to establish trust
- CSR (Certificate Signing Request) — sent to CA to get a certificate signed
- Root of trust — chain of trust starting from a trusted root CA
- Wildcard certificate — covers all subdomains under a domain (*.domain.com)
- CRL (Certificate Revocation List) — list of revoked certs maintained by CA
- OCSP — Online Certificate Status Protocol, checks single cert status in real time
- OCSP Stapling — server embeds OCSP response in TLS handshake, most efficient
- Web of trust — alternative to CA, individuals sign each other's certificates

**Encryption Technologies**
- TPM (Trusted Platform Module) — cryptographic chip on single device, used for
  BitLocker, key generation, password protected
- HSM (Hardware Security Module) — enterprise-scale key management for hundreds
  of servers, clustered for redundancy
- Secure enclave — separate security processor on mobile devices, handles
  encryption, has own boot ROM and true random number generator
- Key management system — centralized management of all keys, auto rotation, logging

**Obfuscation**
- Steganography — hide data inside media (images, audio, video, network traffic)
  security through obscurity, weak on its own
- Covertext — the file containing the hidden data
- Tokenization — replace sensitive data with non-mathematical token, one-time use,
  no encryption needed in transit (credit card payments)
- Data masking — hide portions of data (asterisks on receipts showing last 4 digits)

**Blockchain**
- Distributed ledger — everyone maintains a copy
- Transactions bundled into blocks, hashed for integrity
- If block is modified hash becomes invalid, network rejects it
- Use cases: cryptocurrency, digital identity, supply chain tracking, voting

### 2.1 Threat Actors

| Actor | Internal/External | Motivation | Resources | Sophistication |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Nation state | External | Espionage, disruption, war | Extensive | Very high (APT) |
| Hacktivist | External | Political, philosophical | Low-medium | Medium-high |
| Unskilled attacker | External | Disruption, curiosity | Low | Low (script kiddie) |
| Organized crime | External | Financial gain | High | Medium-high |
| Insider threat | Internal | Revenge, financial gain | Existing access | Medium |
| Shadow IT | Internal | Convenience | Limited | Low |

- APT (Advanced Persistent Threat) — nation state level, constant, multi-target
- Shadow IT — not malicious but bypasses IT controls, unknown security risk
- Insider threat advantage — already knows where data is and how to avoid detection

### 2.2 Threat Vectors and Attack Surfaces

**Messaging-based vectors**
- Email, SMS, instant messages — phishing, malicious links, malware attachments
- Vishing — voice phishing over phone
- Smishing — SMS phishing
- Spear phishing — targeted, uses personal details
- Whaling — spear phishing targeting executives

**Social Engineering**
- Pretexting — fabricated story to extract information
- Impersonation — pretending to be IT help desk, executives, government
- Typosquatting / URL hijacking — domain one typo from legitimate site
- Watering hole attack — compromise third party site target visits, wait for them
  Defense: defense in depth (AV + firewall + IPS layered)
- Misinformation campaigns — fake accounts, amplification via social media algorithms
- Brand impersonation — fake sites using real brand names, indexed by Google

**Other Vectors**
- USB drop attacks — malicious USB in parking lot, can emulate keyboard
- Default credentials — unchanged factory passwords, Mirai botnet exploits these
- Supply chain — compromise vendor/MSP/hardware (Target HVAC 2013,
  SolarWinds Orion 2020, fake Cisco switches 2022)
- Open ports — every enabled service is an attack surface
- Unsupported/legacy systems — no patches available, high risk

### 2.3 Types of Vulnerabilities

**Memory Injection / DLL Injection**
- Malware injects into existing legitimate process memory space
- Inherits process permissions — instant privilege escalation
- Evades signature AV — no standalone malicious process visible
- SOC relevance: behavioral detection catches unusual DLLs in trusted processes

**Buffer Overflow**
- Write beyond memory variable boundary, overflow into adjacent variable
- Can change permission values, execute arbitrary code
- Bounds checking is the developer defense
- Must be repeatable and reliable to be exploitable

**Race Conditions / TOCTOU**
- Race condition: simultaneous events app doesn't account for
- TOCTOU — Time-Of-Check to Time-Of-Use: value changes in the gap between
  check and use
- Examples: bank transfer duplicate funds exploit, Mars rover reboot loop,
  Tesla Model 3 Pwn2Own 2023

**SQL Injection**
- Inject SQL code into unsanitized input field
- OR 1=1 returns all database records
- Can read, modify, delete, crash database
- Defense: input validation
- SOC relevance: OR 1=1 in web logs = injection attempt

**Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)**
- Reflected — attacker sends link to victim, script runs once against trusted site
- Stored/persistent — script posted on site, runs for every visitor
- Commonly JavaScript based
- Defense: input validation on all fields, keep browser updated
- Real example: Subaru non-expiring token gave full vehicle control via XSS

**Hardware Vulnerabilities**
- Firmware — OS inside hardware devices, only manufacturer can patch
- EOL — product no longer sold, patches still available temporarily
- EOSL — no more patches at all, replace immediately
- Legacy devices — mitigate with firewall rules and IPS signatures until replaced

**Virtualization Vulnerabilities**
- VM escape — break out of one VM, access others on same hypervisor
- Resource reuse — hypervisor shares memory between VMs, data leakage risk
- Real example: Pwn2Own 2017 Edge → Windows kernel → VMware chain

**Mobile Device Vulnerabilities**
- Jailbreaking (iOS) / Rooting (Android) — replaces OS, bypasses MDM
- Sideloading — apps installed outside approved store
- MDM bypass is the critical security impact

**Zero-Day Vulnerabilities**
- Unknown to vendor, no patch exists
- Attacker exploits before anyone knows the vulnerability exists
- Defense: defense in depth
- Track at cve.mitre.org

**Supply Chain Vulnerabilities**
- Target 2013 — HVAC contractor → POS network → 40M cards stolen
- SolarWinds 2020 — malicious code in signed Orion update, 18,000 customers
  compromised including Pentagon, Microsoft, Cisco
- Counterfeit Cisco switches 2022 — fake hardware on real networks

**Cloud Vulnerabilities**
- 76% of orgs no MFA on cloud console, 63% of cloud code unpatched
- Common attacks: DoS, auth bypass, directory traversal, SQLi, XSS,
  remote code execution
- Log4j and Spring Cloud Function — easy to exploit, full system access

**Misconfiguration Vulnerabilities**
- Open cloud storage (14M Verizon records exposed 2017)
- Unsecured admin accounts with weak passwords
- Insecure protocols (Telnet, FTP, HTTP) sending data in cleartext
- Default credentials — Mirai botnet scans for 60+ default IoT configs
- Open ports — more services = more attack surface, audit firewall rules regularly

## What I Learned Today
- Asymmetric: encrypt with recipient's public key, decrypt with their private key
- Perfect Forward Secreity — ephemeral session keys protect past sessions
- OCSP Stapling is most efficient cert revocation method — embedded in TLS handshake
- Shadow IT is a threat actor — not malicious but bypasses all security controls
- OR 1=1 in logs is an immediate SQL injection red flag
- Reflected XSS = one victim, stored XSS = everyone who visits
- EOL vs EOSL — EOSL means no more patches, replace now
- Supply chain attacks bypass perimeter by coming through trusted channels
- Defense in depth is the answer to watering hole and zero-day attacks
- Mirai botnet specifically targets default IoT credentials — change them always
